        {
          "post_id": "495341",
          "text": "<p>Let $\\mathbb{N}=\\{1,2,3, \\ldots\\}$ be the set of natural numbers. Let $\\Sigma=\\{a, b\\}$ be an alphabet.<br><br>Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>THE SET OF ALL LANGUAGES OVER $\\Sigma$ IS COUNTABLY INFINITE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>THE SET OF ALL COMPUTABLE FUNCTIONS $f: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{N}$ IS COUNTABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>THE SET OF ALL FINITE SUBSETS OF $\\mathbb{N}$ IS COUNTABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>THE SET OF ALL CONTEXT-FREE GRAMMARS (CFGS) OVER $\\Sigma$ IS UNCOUNTABLE.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "B;C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 123"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "495352",
          "text": "<p>Consider a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA) $M=\\left(Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F\\right)$ where:</p><ul><li>$\\Sigma=\\{a, b\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$Q=\\left\\{S_0, S_a, S_b, S_{e r r}\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$q_0=S_0$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$F=\\left\\{S_0, S_a, S_b\\right\\}$</li></ul><div><div>&nbsp;</div><b id=\"docs-internal-guid-af2222fd-7fff-cea4-6b21-c83bed3eae94\"><img width=\"440\" height=\"169\" alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></b></div><div>&nbsp;</div><div>What is the language recognized by $M$ ?</div><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$\\left\\{w \\in\\{a, b\\}^* \\mid w\\right.$ HAS NO 'aa' SUBSTRING $\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\left\\{w \\in\\{a, b\\}^* \\mid w\\right.$ HAS NO 'bb' SUBSTRING $\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\left\\{w \\in\\{a, b\\}^* \\mid w\\right.$ HAS NO 'aa' SUBSTRING AND NO 'bb' SUBSTRING $\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$\\left\\{w \\in\\{a, b\\}^* \\mid w\\right.$ IS OF THE FORM $(a b)^*$ OR $\\left.(b a)^*\\right\\}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 123"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "495347",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following decision problems:</p><ul><li>(P1) : Given a Context-Free Grammar $G$ and a regular expression $R$, is the language $L(G) \\backslash L(R)$ also a Context-Free Language?<br>&nbsp;</li><li>(P2) : Given a Turing Machine $M$, does $M$ accept at least two distinct prime numbers?</li></ul><p>Which of the following statements is TRUE?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>BOTH (P1) AND (P2) ARE DECIDABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>NEITHER (P1) NOR (P2) IS DECIDABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>ONLY (P1) IS DECIDABLE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>ONLY (P2) IS DECIDABLE.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "subject": "Theory of Computation",
          "day": "Day 123"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "495338",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following languages over the alphabet $\\{a, b, c\\}$ :</p><ul><li>$L_1=\\left\\{w c w \\mid w \\in\\{a, b\\}^*\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_2=\\left\\{w c w^R \\mid w \\in\\{a, b\\}^*\\right\\}$<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_3=\\left\\{w w^R \\mid w \\in\\{a, b\\}^*\\right\\}$</li></ul><p>Which one of the following statements is TRUE?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>$L_2$ IS A CFL, BUT NOT A DETERMINISTIC CFL.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_3$ IS A DETERMINISTIC CFL.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_1$ IS A CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGE.<br>&nbsp;</li><li>$L_2$ IS A DETERMINISTIC CFL, BUT $L_1$ IS NOT A CFL.</li></ol>",
